WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.571
The shared responsibility model explains which security duties are handled by the cloud provider and which duties remain with the customer.

2
00:00:08.691 --> 00:00:24.610
It is one of the most important ideas in cloud security because it prevents two common mistakes: assuming the provider secures everything, or assuming the customer must manage every layer the same way as an on-premises data center.

3
00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:34.118
In general, the provider secures the underlying cloud platform. That includes major parts of the physical facilities, hardware, foundational networking, and service infrastructure.

4
00:00:34.238 --> 00:00:46.074
The customer secures how cloud services are configured and used. That includes identities, roles, policies, data classification, access decisions, network exposure, workload configuration, logging choices, monitoring, and incident response.

5
00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:53.133
The exact boundary depends on the service. Infrastructure services give the customer more control and more responsibility.

6
00:00:53.253 --> 00:01:04.682
If a team runs virtual machines, it usually needs to think about operating system configuration, patching, host firewall settings, installed software, workload identity, secrets, logs, and backup behavior.

7
00:01:04.802 --> 00:01:11.332
Managed services shift some operational work to the provider, but they do not remove customer responsibility.

8
00:01:11.452 --> 00:01:24.513
A managed database may reduce the need to manage the database host, but the customer still needs to control access, protect data, configure network reachability, manage keys, review logs, and understand retention.

9
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:29.940
Shared responsibility does not mean shared confusion. Each system should have clear ownership.

10
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:41.896
Someone should know who owns the resource, who owns the data, who can change access, who reviews logs, who handles exceptions, and who responds if a risky configuration appears.

11
00:01:42.016 --> 00:01:48.547
In many organizations, responsibility is split across platform teams, application teams, security teams, and governance teams.

12
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:53.973
That can work well, but only if the boundaries are documented and practiced.

13
00:01:54.093 --> 00:02:02.256
A useful review question is, what could the provider reasonably know or control, and what can only the customer decide?

14
00:02:02.376 --> 00:02:07.682
The provider cannot know which identity should have access to a business dataset.

15
00:02:07.802 --> 00:02:13.517
The provider cannot know whether a storage bucket contains public documentation or confidential records.

16
00:02:13.637 --> 00:02:19.759
The provider cannot decide which application port should be internet-facing for a specific service.

17
00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:25.185
Those are customer decisions, even when the provider offers tools to implement them.

18
00:02:25.305 --> 00:02:38.775
Treat the shared responsibility model as a map for ownership. For every cloud resource, identify the service model, the provider-managed layer, the customer-managed configuration, and the team accountable for daily operation.

19
00:02:38.895 --> 00:02:48.282
The model should reduce gaps, not create excuses. Secure cloud work starts when each team understands both the provider's role and its own.
